"""Prepare three venue-specific manuscripts from the verified research edition."""
from pathlib import Path
import re,shutil,json
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];base=(R/'workshop/paper.tex').read_text();out=R/'submissions'
# Preserve research edition; each submission has an independent final source.
base=base.replace('We do not claim either idea as new. ','')
base=base.replace('Our specific theoretical increment is the exact local minimum discrepancy within the raw increment-reward family. We found no matching coefficient in these inspected sources; this is a scoped literature comparison, not a proof of priority.','Our question is more specific: how much policy discrepancy remains after the best scalar calibration of the raw increment reward? The next section gives its exact leading coefficient.')
base=base.replace('This isolates a reward comparison; it does not reproduce their architecture, data or reported performance.','The common environment isolates the effect of the reward; the architecture and data differ from those in their study.')
base=base.replace('which cannot be silently equated to an excess-return increment','rather than an excess-return increment')
base=base.replace('These numbers are numerically verified comparisons with a specified preference, not evidence that the other preferences are invalid.','The differences measure departure from the specified MV preference; log utility represents a different investment objective.')
base=base.replace('The use of $u^*$ makes joint calibration an explicit reference-based certificate, not a faster way to compute an unknown reference.','Joint calibration uses $u^*$ and therefore serves as a certificate of alignment with a known reference.')
base=base.replace('This construction specialises established equivalent-objective ideas\\ \\cite{bkm2016}; it is not potential-based shaping preserving the original raw-reward policy.','This construction specialises the equivalent-objective approach of Bj\\"ork et al.\\ \\cite{bkm2016} and changes the optimum of the original raw reward.')
base=base.replace('The identity remains a valid optimality argument on any feasible set with a global maximiser; convexity and positive definite covariance ensure uniqueness and computational tractability here. It follows the existing auxiliary-variable representation\\ \\cite{zhang2021}, not a new generic mean--variance transformation.','The argument applies to any feasible set with a global maximiser. Convexity and positive definite covariance ensure uniqueness here. The identity follows the mean auxiliary used in MVPI\\ \\cite{zhang2021}.')
base=base.replace('No advantage over exact MVPI is claimed.','This provides a constructive comparator with the same optimum.')
base=base.replace('These choices were recorded before inspecting strategy outcomes.','The experimental protocol fixed these choices before strategy outcomes were inspected.')
base=base.replace('This is a contextual direct-gradient optimisation diagnostic, not a predictive neural agent or reproduction of PPO, DDPG or the recent architectures.','Independent logits isolate optimisation error on each fitted state. They do not test generalisation across states, as a predictive neural policy would.')
base=base.replace('The strongest contribution is the exact fixed-horizon scalar-mismatch coefficient together with this explicit historical alignment audit. The work does not establish a new state-of-the-art trading algorithm. Its value for interpretable and aligned financial models is to make the economic meaning of a reward testable, including cases where successful optimisation or attractive Sharpe obscures the intended preference. Extending the exact audit to estimated dependent terminal equilibria and investable point-in-time assets remains a distinct research problem.','Scalar tuning has a precise limit in the independent terminal-wealth problem. With constraints and turnover costs, joint calibration restores the fitted one-period preference, while the realised outcome still depends on estimation quality. These results make reward semantics testable before interpreting a learned policy or comparing backtests. Extending the audit to dependent terminal equilibria and point-in-time investable portfolios would require a richer reference model.')
disclosure=r'''\section*{Acknowledgments}
OpenAI Codex assisted with the research and preparation of this manuscript. Its use covered literature searches, mathematical derivations, code, experiment analysis, drafting and revision. Reported numerical results were computed from the archived experiments; figures were plotted from those outputs.

'''
def segment(s,start,end):return s[s.index(start):s.index(end)]
def replace_section(s,start,end,new):return s[:s.index(start)]+new+'\n\n'+s[s.index(end):]
def abstract(s,new):return re.sub(r'\\begin\{abstract\}.*?\\end\{abstract\}',lambda _:r'\begin{abstract}'+'\n'+new+'\n'+r'\end{abstract}',s,flags=re.S)
def title(s,new):return re.sub(r'\\title\{[^\n]*\}',lambda _:r'\title{'+new+'}',s,count=1)
def tail(s,new):return s[:s.index(r'\section{Implications and scope}')]+new+'\n\n'+s[s.index(r'\clearpage'):]
def conference(s,short,long):return re.sub(r'\\acmConference[^\n]*',lambda _:r'\acmConference['+short+r" '26]{"+long+r'}{November 2026}{Milan, Italy}',s,count=1)
# 1: Interpretation and exact alignment, with the complete horizon proof.
iafm=title(base,'When Portfolio Rewards Miss the Target: Exact Mismatch and Policy Alignment')
iafm=abstract(iafm,r'''A risk-sensitive portfolio reward need not implement the investor's stated objective. We examine this difference in two settings. For independent returns, we derive the exact leading policy discrepancy that remains after optimally calibrating a quadratic wealth-increment reward: a common scalar penalty cannot recover terminal mean--variance equilibrium when interest is nonzero and the horizon exceeds one period. For constrained allocation with turnover costs, the mean--variance auxiliary identity shows why risk and cost coefficients must be calibrated together. A walk-forward study of ten US industry portfolios over 432 months confirms fitted-policy recovery across 17 settings. Scalar calibration and a log-minus-variance reward retain allocation differences, while an adapted mean--variance policy iteration method matches the reference. Better alignment does not yield a statistically clear realised performance gain. The analysis separates the economic meaning of a reward from optimisation accuracy and investment performance, allowing each to be checked directly.''')
iafm=replace_section(iafm,r'\section{Introduction}',r'\section{Related work and comparison design}',r'''\section{Introduction}
Suppose an investor asks for mean return minus a variance penalty. Replacing that preference with return minus a squared-return penalty looks harmless, but it also penalises the square of the mean. A learner can optimise the resulting reward accurately and still choose the wrong policy for the stated preference. Interest, trading constraints and transaction costs make the distinction harder to resolve by tuning one coefficient.

Financial learning uses several legitimate objectives. Jiang et al.\ \cite{jiang2025} combine log return and variance; Simon et al.\ \cite{simon2026} optimise investor utility directly; Cong et al.\ \cite{cong2026} allow whole-trajectory objectives. Their coexistence makes the reference objective essential: policy disagreement is meaningful only after the intended preference has been specified.

We study the map from reward to policy. First, an independent-return benchmark gives an exact expression for the discrepancy that remains after the best scalar calibration of a wealth-increment reward. Second, a constrained historical study shows how a risk adjustment also changes the effective turnover penalty. Joint calibration restores the fitted preference through the established mean auxiliary used in MVPI\ \cite{zhang2021}. The historical test then asks a separate question: does this alignment improve realised outcomes?

The answer is instructive. Calibration recovers the fitted policy, yet the main realised MV differences are not statistically clear. A useful explanation of a portfolio learner should therefore identify its induced preference as well as its prediction or backtest performance.''')
# Shared shortened empirical versions; no dangling terminal-theorem references.
def empirical(s):
 s=replace_section(s,r'\section{An exact test of terminal-objective alignment}',r'\section{Constrained alignment with turnover costs}','')
 s=s.replace('Historical implementation adds constraints and costs. On a fixed past-only estimated state, let $C$ be the simplex, $p$ the pre-trade allocation and $c(u)=\\|u-p\\|_1$. Define the fitted one-period preference','Let $m$ and $\\Sigma\\succ0$ be estimated excess-return moments available before trading, $M=\\Sigma+mm^\\top$, and $\\nu=m^\\top M^{-1}m$. The feasible set $C$ is the simplex, $p$ is the pre-trade allocation, and $c(u)=\\|u-p\\|_1$. For risk aversion $\\varphi>0$ and cost coefficient $\\lambda\\ge0$, define')
 s=s.replace('This section does not extend Theorem~\\ref{th:mismatch} to estimated or dependent markets.','The reference specifies a one-period decision under estimated moments.')
 return s
related=r'''\section{Portfolio objectives and validation}
Direct utility optimisation is established in financial learning. Simon et al.\ \cite{simon2026}, Section 4.1, Eq.\ (17), use centred mean--variance preferences, with turnover costs in Section 3.6. Their deep portfolio policy and US stock-characteristic data differ from the common industry-portfolio environment used here. Cong et al.\ \cite{cong2026}, Eqs.\ (19)--(20), optimise trajectory objectives through pathwise gradients. These approaches show that objective-aware learning is feasible.

Other rewards encode different preferences. Jiang et al.\ \cite{jiang2025}, Eq.\ (8), use net log return minus a variance penalty; Eq.\ (19) adds trading restrictions. We adapt that preference using monthly returns, hard simplex constraints and a first-order turnover charge. The comparison changes the reward while holding the estimated state fixed; it does not reproduce their CNN/WaveNet DPG system. Their earlier TD3 study\ \cite{jiang2024}, Eq.\ (4), combines portfolio value, variance and trading costs, motivating explicit treatment of all three terms.

The calibration below follows the mean auxiliary in MVPI\ \cite{zhang2021}. Known-policy validation also has precedents: Chaouki et al.\ \cite{chaouki2020}, Section 3, compare learned and analytical controls, while Gollart and Okhrin\ \cite{gollart2025}, Section 4.2.1 and Table 1, align terminal utility with a DDPG problem. Our contribution is a reproducible test of risk--cost preference drift and a comparison of its magnitude with optimisation error and historical uncertainty.'''
rai=conference(empirical(base),'RAIOps4Fin','Workshop on Applied Responsible AI Operations for Finance')
rai=title(rai,'Validating Portfolio Rewards: Preference Drift, Optimisation Error and Historical Performance')
rai=abstract(rai,r'''A portfolio model can pass an optimisation check while implementing a different risk preference from the one it was designed to follow. We develop a validation procedure that compares the financial reference, the exact reward optimum and the finite-budget policy on the same pre-trade states. For constrained mean--variance allocation, an auxiliary-variable identity shows that calibrating a squared-return penalty also changes the effective turnover cost. Joint calibration restores the fitted reference; risk-only calibration generally does not. We implement the procedure on ten US industry portfolios from 1990 to 2025, with 432 monthly decisions and 17 robustness settings. The audit distinguishes three findings: fitted-policy recovery is numerically precise, finite-budget optimisation error can exceed reward-design error, and realised performance gains remain statistically inconclusive. The resulting records make preference fidelity and solver accuracy inspectable without treating either as evidence of investment superiority.''')
rai=replace_section(rai,r'\section{Introduction}',r'\section{Related work and comparison design}',r'''\section{Introduction}
A validation report for a learned allocator should answer a simple question: what preference does the deployed reward implement? Return, variance, squared return and turnover penalties can look similar in a specification while inducing different trades. A successful optimiser establishes only that the specified reward has been optimised. A favourable backtest answers a different question again.

We turn this distinction into a reproducible model-validation procedure. The procedure stores the intended objective, the reward-induced allocation, numerical optimality certificates and subsequent realised returns. Comparisons use common pre-trade states so that a reward change is not confused with differences accumulated along two trading paths.

Our case study is a constrained mean--variance allocator with proportional turnover costs. The mean auxiliary in MVPI\ \cite{zhang2021} gives an exact identity: adjusting the quadratic risk coefficient changes the effective cost preference unless the turnover coefficient is adjusted too. This supplies a concrete test case with a known fitted reference.

The historical evidence demonstrates both the use and the limit of the procedure. Joint calibration matches the reference across 17 settings, but the main realised performance intervals include zero. The contribution is an implemented validation method with independently checked records, rather than a claim of production deployment or regulatory certification.''')
rai=replace_section(rai,r'\section{Related work and comparison design}',r'\section{Constrained alignment with turnover costs}',related)
operational=r'''\section{A reproducible validation record}
For each decision date, the record stores the estimation window, preference coefficients, pre-trade allocation, chosen weights, realised return and numerical certificate. The raw data snapshots have recorded hashes. Reconstructing the common state and cost calculation from these records provides a check independent of the strategy runner.

Three comparisons have distinct interpretations. A nonzero gap between the reward optimum and the fitted reference identifies preference drift. A gap between a finite-budget policy and its own reward optimum identifies optimisation error. A difference in subsequent realised returns measures investment outcomes under estimation uncertainty. Combining these quantities into one score would obscure which part of the model requires attention.

The numerical tolerance is an implementation choice, not a business acceptance threshold. Quadratic iterates are accepted only after a feasible-set first-order certificate is at most $2\times10^{-7}$; the largest saved common-state certificate in the primary run is $4.27\times10^{-9}$. Risk-only calibration has mean fitted regret $3.35\times10^{-7}$, so the recorded numerical precision matters when interpreting such small effects. A model owner must separately decide whether the allocation discrepancy is economically material.

The implementation checks all 4,320 primary strategy decisions for lagged estimation dates, simplex feasibility, drift, cost accounting and common-state regret. The research test suite contains 31 passing tests, alongside ten original verification checks. These checks support the reported calculations; they do not establish live execution quality or a complete operational control system.'''
rai=tail(rai,operational+r'''
\section{Discussion}
The audit detects a specific preference change that aggregate performance can conceal. It also prevents overinterpreting its repair: minimum variance beats the fitted target in the primary historical sample, and the paired MV intervals do not establish a calibration advantage. Estimation risk remains after reward alignment has been resolved.

The procedure is suitable for a model-development validation record. Applying it in production would additionally require an agreed financial reference, point-in-time investable data, realistic execution costs and monitoring thresholds chosen for the mandate. Under return dependence, a terminal equilibrium also requires a continuation model; the rolling one-period reference used here does not supply one.''')
# 3: Turnover and trading-model preferences; explicitly bounded relevance.
micro=conference(empirical(base),'AI-Driven Markets','AI-Driven Market Microstructure in Centralized and Decentralized Finance')
micro=title(micro,'Risk and Turnover Preferences in Learned Portfolio Rebalancing')
micro=abstract(micro,r'''Risk and turnover penalties jointly determine the trades selected by a portfolio reward. Replacing variance with a second moment changes this trade-off, even when the risk coefficient is subsequently calibrated. For a constrained one-period allocator, we derive the additional turnover penalty introduced by risk-only calibration and show that jointly scaling risk and cost coefficients preserves the mean--variance optimum. The result follows the standard mean auxiliary and is verified against an adapted MVPI solver. A walk-forward study of ten US industry portfolios over 1990--2025 compares induced allocations on common pre-trade states and realised returns on separate strategy paths. Joint calibration recovers the fitted policy across 17 settings; scalar and risk-only adjustments retain discrepancies. Realised performance differences are inconclusive, and finite-budget policy optimisation introduces a larger error than the primary reward-design gap. The study provides an interpretable test of risk--cost preferences in trading models, with monthly proportional costs rather than an order-book or market-impact model.''')
micro=replace_section(micro,r'\section{Introduction}',r'\section{Related work and comparison design}',r'''\section{Introduction}
A trading reward balances the incentive to take risk against the incentive to trade. These incentives are often assigned separate coefficients, yet changing one part of the reward can alter the effective meaning of the other. In particular, replacing a variance penalty with a squared-return penalty and then recalibrating risk need not preserve the intended turnover preference.

This paper studies that interaction in constrained portfolio rebalancing. On each estimated market state, the intended reference is mean return minus covariance risk and proportional turnover cost. We compare it with second-moment rewards, scalar calibrations and a log-minus-variance preference adapted from Jiang et al.\ \cite{jiang2025}. All local comparisons face the same pre-trade portfolio.

An auxiliary-variable identity makes the distortion explicit. Risk-only calibration adds a turnover term whose magnitude depends on the target expected return. Joint risk--cost calibration removes it and agrees with an adapted MVPI method\ \cite{zhang2021}. Historical data then show how this exact fitted relation differs from realised investment performance.

The setting addresses the robustness and interpretation of AI trading objectives. It uses monthly industry portfolios and a first-order proportional cost approximation. It therefore identifies a reward-level source of preference drift before adding execution dynamics; it does not estimate market impact, liquidity provision or order-book behaviour.''')
micro=replace_section(micro,r'\section{Related work and comparison design}',r'\section{Constrained alignment with turnover costs}',related)
micro=tail(micro,r'''\section{Implications for trading models}
The risk and turnover coefficients should be interpreted together. Equation~\eqref{eq:costdistortion} identifies the term left behind by calibrating risk alone. When the target expected return is positive, the fitted objective places extra weight on avoiding turnover. This interpretation concerns a fixed pre-trade state; it does not guarantee less trading along a strategy's realised path.

The historical comparison supports the algebraic correction but not a performance ranking in its favour. Raw, scalar and risk-only rewards produce small primary allocation differences, and their paired MV comparisons with joint calibration include zero. Minimum variance performs better than the fitted target in the primary sample. Reward fidelity and the accuracy of moment estimates therefore remain separate concerns.

In execution or market-making models, inventory risk, impact and fill uncertainty introduce further terms. The present result suggests checking the induced trade-off whenever a quadratic reward is rescaled, but it does not establish the corresponding identity for endogenous costs or liquidity. Those extensions require an execution-level model and data. Within the proportional-cost allocation setting, the common-state audit provides a direct, reproducible test of the preference encoded by a reward.''')
for key,s in [('iafm',iafm),('raiops4fin',rai),('market-microstructure',micro)]:
 d=out/key;d.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
 s=s.replace('anonymous,review,pbalance','anonymous,review')
 s=s.replace(r'\begin{figure*}[tp]',r'\begin{figure*}[p]')
 s=s.replace(r'\clearpage',disclosure+r'\clearpage',1)
 (d/'paper.tex').write_text(s)
 for name in ['references.bib','results_table.tex','acmart.cls','ACM-Reference-Format.bst']:shutil.copy2(R/'workshop'/name,d/name)
 (d/'figures').mkdir(exist_ok=True)
 for p in (R/'workshop/figures').glob('*.pdf'):shutil.copy2(p,d/'figures'/p.name)
 print(key,len(s.split()),'source words')
